00:42:02,967 --> 00:42:07,279
<i>All units. Ford suspect vehicle has</i>
<i>been found in a parking structure</i>...

226
00:42:48,887 --> 00:42:53,278
Reese, why me?

227
00:42:55,247 --> 00:42:57,841
Why does it want me?

228
00:43:03,047 --> 00:43:05,515
There was a nuclear war.

229
00:43:07,007 --> 00:43:09,202
A few years from now,

230
00:43:11,207 --> 00:43:16,645
all this, this whole place, everything,

231
00:43:16,727 --> 00:43:21,164
it's gone, just gone.

232
00:43:21,887 --> 00:43:23,878
There were survivors.

233
00:43:23,967 --> 00:43:25,958
Here, there.

234
00:43:28,487 --> 00:43:31,479
Nobody even knew who started it.

235
00:43:33,527 --> 00:43:38,157
- It was the machines, Sarah.
- I don't understand.

236
00:43:38,247 --> 00:43:40,715
Defence network computers.

237
00:43:41,407 --> 00:43:44,399
New, powerful,

238
00:43:45,487 --> 00:43:50,083
hooked into everything,
trusted to run it all.

239
00:43:50,167 --> 00:43:54,558
They say it got smart -
a new order of intelligence.

240
00:43:56,127 --> 00:44:01,121
Then it saw all people as a threat.
Not just the ones on the other side.

241
00:44:01,207 --> 00:44:04,005
It decided our fate in a microsecond.

242
00:44:05,407 --> 00:44:07,398
Extermination.

243
00:44:20,967 --> 00:44:23,162
Did you see this war?

244
00:44:34,367 --> 00:44:39,043
No. I grew up after. In the ruins.

245
00:44:39,127 --> 00:44:42,119
Starving. Hiding from the H-Ks.

246
00:44:42,207 --> 00:44:45,005
- H-Ks?
- Hunter-killers.

247
00:44:45,087 --> 00:44:48,875
Patrol machines
built in automated factories.

248
00:44:48,967 --> 00:44:53,438
Most of us were rounded up.
Put in camps for orderly disposal.

249
00:44:54,727 --> 00:44:57,287
This was burned in by laser scanner.

250
00:45:00,967 --> 00:45:04,960
Some of us were kept alive. To work.

251
00:45:06,087 --> 00:45:08,555
Loading bodies.

252
00:45:08,647 --> 00:45:11,241
The disposal units ran night and day.

253
00:45:11,327 --> 00:45:14,319
We were that close to going out for ever.

254
00:45:17,407 --> 00:45:20,797
But there was one man
who taught us to fight.

255
00:45:20,887 --> 00:45:22,878
To storm the wire of the camps.

256
00:45:22,967 --> 00:45:26,437
To smash those metal
motherfuckers into junk.

257
00:45:27,607 --> 00:45:30,519
He turned it around.
He brought us back from the brink.

258
00:45:30,607 --> 00:45:34,805
His name was Connor. John Connor.

259
00:45:34,887 --> 00:45:39,358
Your son, Sarah. Your unborn son.

260
00:47:24,647 --> 00:47:26,319
Drive.

261
00:47:32,327 --> 00:47:34,966
Reese!

262
00:47:52,367 --> 00:47:55,723
No, Reese. No, no, no. They'll kill you.

263
00:47:55,807 --> 00:47:59,482
You in the Cadillac,
let me see your hands now.

264
00:48:01,807 --> 00:48:04,196
Get out of the car.

265
00:48:06,527 --> 00:48:08,518
Drop to your knees.

266
00:48:08,607 --> 00:48:11,804
- Out of the car.
- Drop to the ground.

267
00:48:38,967 --> 00:48:43,165
Sarah. Sarah, try to drink some of this.

268
00:48:45,767 --> 00:48:47,758
Listen, are you sure it's them?

269
00:48:47,847 --> 00:48:50,759
I mean,
maybe I should look at the bodies.

270
00:48:50,847 --> 00:48:54,920
No. They've been identified
and there's no doubt.

271
00:48:57,367 --> 00:49:01,804
Ginger. Kiddo.

272
00:49:02,247 --> 00:49:07,321
- Sarah, this is Dr Silberman.
- Hi, Sarah.

273
00:49:07,407 --> 00:49:11,320
Tell him everything Reese told you,
Sarah. Do you feel up to that?

274
00:49:11,407 --> 00:49:13,398
Yeah, I guess so.

275
00:49:15,127 --> 00:49:18,961
- You're a doctor?
- Criminal psychologist.

276
00:49:19,047 --> 00:49:23,677
- Is Reese crazy?
- That's what we're gonna find out.

277
00:50:44,900 --> 00:50:48,495
So you're a soldier. Fighting for whom?

278
00:50:49,580 --> 00:50:54,938
With the 132nd under Perry.
From '21 to '27.

279
00:50:55,020 --> 00:50:57,739
That's the year 2027?

280
00:50:57,820 --> 00:50:59,811
<i>That's right. Then I was assigned</i>...

281
00:50:59,900 --> 00:51:03,575
- This is fucking great.
...<i>under John Connor</i>.

282
00:51:03,660 --> 00:51:06,652
- Who was the enemy?
- A computer defence system...

283
00:51:06,740 --> 00:51:08,970
Goddamn! Sorry.

284
00:51:09,060 --> 00:51:12,450
...built for SAC-NORAD
by Cyberdyne Systems.

285
00:51:12,540 --> 00:51:14,531
I see.

286
00:51:14,620 --> 00:51:20,217
And this computer thinks it can win
by killing the mother of its enemy.

287
00:51:20,300 --> 00:51:23,497
Killing him, in effect,
before he's even conceived.

288
00:51:23,580 --> 00:51:25,775
A sort of retroactive abortion?

289
00:51:27,180 --> 00:51:30,570
Silberman cracks me up.
Last week, a guy burned his Afghan.

290
00:51:30,660 --> 00:51:32,696
- He screwed it first...
- Ed, shut up.

291
00:51:32,780 --> 00:51:35,374
Why didn't the computer
just kill Connor then?

292
00:51:35,460 --> 00:51:37,928
Why this elaborate scheme
with the Terminator?

293
00:51:38,020 --> 00:51:42,332
It had no choice.
Their defence grid was smashed.

294
00:51:42,420 --> 00:51:46,652
We'd won. Taking out Connor then
would make no difference.

295
00:51:46,740 --> 00:51:49,573
Skynet had to wipe out
his entire existence.

296
00:51:49,660 --> 00:51:54,290
Is that when you captured the lab
complex and found... What is it called?

297
00:51:54,380 --> 00:51:56,769
The time displacement equipment?

298
00:51:56,860 --> 00:52:00,569
That's right. The Terminator
had already gone through.

299
00:52:00,660 --> 00:52:03,618
Connor sent me to intercept
and they blew the whole place.

300
00:52:03,700 --> 00:52:06,294
Well, how are you supposed to get back?

301
00:52:06,380 --> 00:52:08,371
I can't.

302
00:52:09,380 --> 00:52:14,374
Nobody goes home.
Nobody else comes through.

303
00:52:15,460 --> 00:52:18,850
It's just him and me.

304
00:53:58,940 --> 00:54:01,408
<i>It's just him and me</i>.

305
00:54:04,300 --> 00:54:09,249
<i>Why didn't you bring any weapons?</i>
<i>Something more advanced</i>.

306
00:54:09,340 --> 00:54:12,138
<i>Don't you have ray guns?</i>

307
00:54:12,220 --> 00:54:15,656
- Ray guns.
<i>- Show me a piece of future technology</i>.

308
00:54:15,740 --> 00:54:20,256
<i>You go naked. Something about the</i>
<i>field generated by a living organism</i>.

309
00:54:20,340 --> 00:54:23,935
<i>Nothing dead will go</i>.
<i>I didn't build the fucking thing</i>.

310
00:54:24,020 --> 00:54:28,377
<i>OK, OK. But this cyborg, if it's metal</i>... <i>?</i>

311
00:54:28,460 --> 00:54:31,850
<i>- Surrounded by living tissue</i>.
<i>- Oh, right</i>.

312
00:54:31,940 --> 00:54:34,693
Great stuff.
I could make a career out of this guy.

313
00:54:34,780 --> 00:54:37,658
See how clever it is?
It doesn't require a shred of proof.

314
00:54:37,740 --> 00:54:42,211
Most paranoid delusions are intricate,
but this is brilliant.

315
00:54:43,140 --> 00:54:46,849
<i>- Why were the other women killed?</i>
<i>- Records were lost in the war</i>.

316
00:54:46,940 --> 00:54:49,534
<i>Skynet knew almost nothing</i>
<i>about Connor's mother</i>.

317
00:54:49,620 --> 00:54:53,215
<i>Her full name, where she lived</i>.
<i>They just knew the city</i>.

318
00:54:53,300 --> 00:54:56,098
<i>The Terminator was</i>
<i>just being systematic</i>.

319
00:54:56,180 --> 00:54:58,899
<i>- Let's go back</i>...
<i>- Look, you have heard enough</i>.

320
00:54:58,980 --> 00:55:02,211
<i>I have answered your questions</i>.
<i>I have to see Sarah Connor</i>.

321
00:55:02,300 --> 00:55:06,009
<i>- I'm afraid that's not up to me</i>.
<i>- Then why am I talking to you?</i>

322
00:55:06,100 --> 00:55:08,091
<i>- Who is in authority here?</i>
<i>- Please</i>...

323
00:55:08,180 --> 00:55:10,171
<i>Shut up!</i>

324
00:55:10,260 --> 00:55:12,854
<i>You still don't get it, do you?</i>

325
00:55:12,940 --> 00:55:18,014
<i>He'll find her. That's what he does</i>.
<i>That's all he does</i>.

326
00:55:18,100 --> 00:55:23,493
<i>You can't stop him. He'll reach down</i>
<i>her throat and pull her fucking heart out!</i>

327
00:55:23,580 --> 00:55:26,014
- Doc.
<i>- Let go of me!</i>

328
00:55:30,100 --> 00:55:32,091
Sorry.

329
00:55:35,700 --> 00:55:37,292
He's crazy?

330
00:55:37,380 --> 00:55:40,690
In technical terminology, he's a loon.

331
00:55:41,340 --> 00:55:46,539
Sarah, this is what they call body armour.
Our TAC guys wear these.

332
00:55:46,620 --> 00:55:48,815
It'll stop a 12-gauge round.

333
00:55:48,900 --> 00:55:53,018
This other man must've been wearing
one under his coat. Feel that.

334
00:55:54,780 --> 00:55:58,568
- He punched through the windshield.
- He was probably on PCP.

335
00:55:58,660 --> 00:56:01,299
Broke every bone in his hand.
He wouldn't feel it.

336
00:56:01,380 --> 00:56:03,769
There was this guy once...
You see this scar?

337
00:56:03,860 --> 00:56:05,851
Thank you.

338
00:56:05,940 --> 00:56:09,410
There's a couch in this other room.
Stretch out and get some sleep.

339
00:56:09,500 --> 00:56:11,968
Be at least an hour
before your mother gets here.

340
00:56:12,060 --> 00:56:15,132
- I can't sleep.
- Try.

341
00:56:15,220 --> 00:56:19,213
It may not look it,
but that couch is very comfortable.

342
00:56:20,460 --> 00:56:24,453
You'll be perfectly safe.
We got 30 cops in this building.

343
00:56:25,460 --> 00:56:27,451
Thank you.

344
00:56:32,980 --> 00:56:34,971
- Good night.
- Good night.

345
00:56:48,820 --> 00:56:52,369
I'm a friend of Sarah Connor.
I was told she's here. Could I see her?

346
00:56:52,460 --> 00:56:54,928
No. Can't see her.
She's making a statement.

347
00:56:55,020 --> 00:56:56,612
Where is she?

348
00:56:56,700 --> 00:57:01,296
It make take a while. If you wanna wait,
there's a bench over there.

349
00:57:10,540 --> 00:57:12,371
I'll be back.

350
00:58:05,660 --> 00:58:07,651
Stay here.

351
00:58:45,300 --> 00:58:47,291
Watch him.

352
00:59:51,740 --> 00:59:53,537
Ed...

353
01:00:20,620 --> 01:00:23,214
Sarah!

354
01:00:23,300 --> 01:00:25,291
Reese.

355
01:00:28,260 --> 01:00:29,659
This way.

356
01:01:08,620 --> 01:01:11,088
<i>KFLB news time. 4.36</i>.

357
01:01:11,180 --> 01:01:14,377
<i>Top story - the largest single</i>
<i>law enforcement mobilisation</i>

358
01:01:14,460 --> 01:01:17,452
<i>in California history</i>
<i>is currently underway</i>.

359
01:01:17,540 --> 01:01:21,294
<i>Police in five counties are engaged</i>
<i>in a massive manhunt for</i>...

360
01:01:24,580 --> 01:01:26,571
Take this.

361
01:01:28,860 --> 01:01:31,454
All right. Let's get this off the road.

362
01:02:01,100 --> 01:02:04,251
- You cold?
- Freezing.

363
01:02:17,460 --> 01:02:19,451
Reese?

364
01:02:20,300 --> 01:02:22,689
- You got a first name?
- Kyle.

365
01:02:23,980 --> 01:02:28,178
Kyle, what's it like
when you go through time?

366
01:02:30,820 --> 01:02:32,811
White light.

367
01:02:34,900 --> 01:02:36,891
Pain.

368
01:02:39,420 --> 01:02:43,413
- It's like being born maybe.
- My God.

369
01:02:44,220 --> 01:02:46,211
I caught one back there.

370
01:02:47,540 --> 01:02:50,293
- You mean you got shot?
- It's not bad.

371
01:02:51,060 --> 01:02:54,655
- We gotta get you to a doctor.
- It's OK. Forget it.

372
01:02:54,740 --> 01:02:59,495
What do you mean, forget it?
Are you crazy? Take this off.

373
01:03:10,940 --> 01:03:12,737
Jesus.

374
01:03:12,820 --> 01:03:15,334
See? It passed right through the meat.

375
01:03:15,420 --> 01:03:17,775
This is gonna make me puke.

376
01:03:17,860 --> 01:03:20,932
- Would you just talk about something?
- What?

377
01:03:21,020 --> 01:03:25,411
I don't know. Anything.
Just talk. Tell me about my son.

378
01:03:26,180 --> 01:03:28,171
He's about my height.

379
01:03:31,220 --> 01:03:33,415
He has your eyes.

380
01:03:36,340 --> 01:03:38,331
What's he like?

381
01:03:38,420 --> 01:03:42,015
You trust him. He's got a strength.

382
01:03:43,580 --> 01:03:45,650
I'd die for John Connor.

383
01:03:48,300 --> 01:03:51,770
Well, at least now I know
what to name him.

384
01:03:52,860 --> 01:03:57,456
Don't suppose you know who the father is
so I won't tell him to get lost?

385
01:03:57,540 --> 01:04:00,338
John never said much about him.

386
01:04:00,420 --> 01:04:03,412
- I know he dies before the war...
- Wait.

387
01:04:04,780 --> 01:04:07,374
I don't wanna know.

388
01:04:07,460 --> 01:04:10,770
So was it John that sent you here?

389
01:04:10,860 --> 01:04:13,454
I volunteered.

390
01:04:13,540 --> 01:04:18,216
- Why?
- It was a chance to meet the legend.

391
01:04:18,300 --> 01:04:24,899
Sarah Connor,
who taught her son to fight, organise,

392
01:04:24,980 --> 01:04:27,096
prepare from when he was a kid.

393
01:04:27,180 --> 01:04:30,775
When you were in hiding before the war.

394
01:04:33,060 --> 01:04:36,416
You're talking about things
I haven't done yet in the past tense.

395
01:04:36,500 --> 01:04:38,968
It's driving me crazy.

396
01:04:42,100 --> 01:04:44,489
Are you sure you have the right person?

397
01:04:44,580 --> 01:04:46,696
I'm sure.

398
01:04:46,780 --> 01:04:50,568
Come on! Do I look like
the mother of the future?

399
01:04:52,260 --> 01:04:57,050
I mean, am I tough? Organised?
I can't even balance my chequebook.

400
01:05:00,020 --> 01:05:03,808
Look, Reese, I didn't ask
for this honour and I don't want it.

401
01:05:03,900 --> 01:05:06,175
Any of it.

402
01:05:08,220 --> 01:05:13,169
Your son gave me a message
to give to you. Made me memorise it.

403
01:05:13,260 --> 01:05:16,252
Thank you, Sarah,
for your courage through the dark years.

404
01:05:16,340 --> 01:05:20,856
I can't help you with what you must
soon face. The future is not set.

405
01:05:20,940 --> 01:05:23,408
You must be stronger than
you imagine you can be.

406
01:05:23,500 --> 01:05:26,731
You must survive or I will never exist.

407
01:05:26,820 --> 01:05:28,811
That's all.

408
01:05:31,500 --> 01:05:34,219
It's a good field dressing.

409
01:05:34,300 --> 01:05:36,291
You like it?

410
01:05:36,380 --> 01:05:38,575
It's my first.

411
01:05:41,940 --> 01:05:44,534
Get some sleep. It'll be light soon.

412
01:05:48,380 --> 01:05:50,336
OK.

413
01:05:59,740 --> 01:06:01,935
Talk to me some more.

414
01:06:04,700 --> 01:06:07,134
About what?

415
01:06:07,220 --> 01:06:09,814
Tell me about where you're from.

416
01:06:11,700 --> 01:06:13,691
All right.

417
01:06:14,980 --> 01:06:19,371
You stay down by day,
but at night you can move around.

418
01:06:19,460 --> 01:06:24,454
You still have to be careful
because the H-Ks use infrared.

419
01:06:25,100 --> 01:06:30,493
But they're not too bright.
John taught us ways to dust them.

420
01:06:31,300 --> 01:06:33,894
That's when the infiltrators
started to appear.

421
01:06:33,980 --> 01:06:37,575
The Terminators were the newest.
The worst.

422
01:06:46,740 --> 01:06:51,939
<i>Traversing your sector in search mode</i>.
<i>750 metres north of your position</i>.

423
01:06:56,260 --> 01:06:58,251
Let's go.

424
01:07:40,900 --> 01:07:43,972
- Reese. DN384...
- Right. Let him in.

425
01:09:12,300 --> 01:09:15,019
Terminator!

426
01:10:28,380 --> 01:10:31,417
I was dreaming about dogs.

427
01:10:31,500 --> 01:10:34,492
We use 'em to spot Terminators.

428
01:10:35,180 --> 01:10:38,331
Your world is pretty terrifying.

429
01:11:14,660 --> 01:11:18,448
Hey, buddy,
you got a dead cat in there or what?

430
01:11:25,500 --> 01:11:27,491
Fuck you, asshole.

431
01:11:45,660 --> 01:11:47,457
God damn.

432
01:11:48,260 --> 01:11:49,852
Thank you.

433
01:11:54,980 --> 01:11:57,016
Is this enough?

434
01:11:57,100 --> 01:12:01,093
Yeah, it's enough.
I don't wanna ask where you got it.

435
01:12:06,100 --> 01:12:08,091
- I'm coming.
- We need a room.

436
01:12:08,180 --> 01:12:11,217
- With a kitchen.
- Do you have one with a kitchen?

437
01:12:26,740 --> 01:12:29,129
I am dying for a shower.

438
01:12:30,820 --> 01:12:33,414
We should check your bandage, too.

439
01:12:33,500 --> 01:12:36,970
Later. I'm going out for supplies.

440
01:12:40,660 --> 01:12:42,651
Keep this.

441
01:13:06,540 --> 01:13:09,976
No, I can't tell you where I am, Mom.
I was told not to say.

442
01:13:10,060 --> 01:13:12,096
<i>I need to know where to reach you</i>.

443
01:13:12,180 --> 01:13:17,208
<i>You tell me to hide out at the cabin</i>
<i>and you won't tell me what's going on?</i>

444
01:13:17,300 --> 01:13:19,495
<i>I am worried sick here</i>.

445
01:13:20,220 --> 01:13:23,974
OK. OK, here's the number.

446
01:13:24,060 --> 01:13:26,290
<i>- You ready?</i>
- Yes. Go ahead.

447
01:13:26,380 --> 01:13:32,694
<i>It's 4085551439. Room nine</i>.

448
01:13:32,780 --> 01:13:34,611
- <i>Got it?</i>
- I've got it.

449
01:13:34,700 --> 01:13:38,932
<i>I gotta go. I'm sorry</i>
<i>I can't tell you more right now</i>.

450
01:13:39,020 --> 01:13:41,011
<i>I love you, Mom</i>.

451
01:13:43,980 --> 01:13:46,574
I love you, too, sweetheart.

452
01:13:58,020 --> 01:14:00,488
<i>- Tiki Motel</i>.
- Give me your address there.

453
01:14:03,980 --> 01:14:05,971
What have we got?

454
01:14:08,540 --> 01:14:14,376
Mothballs... corn syrup... ammonia.

455
01:14:14,460 --> 01:14:16,655
What's for dinner?

456
01:14:16,740 --> 01:14:19,812
- Plastique.
- That sounds good.

457
01:14:19,900 --> 01:14:21,891
What is it?

458
01:14:21,980 --> 01:14:25,450
It's a nitroglycerin base.
It's a bit more stable.

459
01:14:25,540 --> 01:14:28,338
I learned to make it when I was a kid.

460
01:14:38,860 --> 01:14:41,454
Make sure there's none on the threads.

461
01:14:42,900 --> 01:14:44,891
Like this.

462
01:14:46,260 --> 01:14:48,455
Screw the end cap on.

463
01:14:49,860 --> 01:14:52,055
Very gently.

464
01:14:53,980 --> 01:14:56,369
You must've had a fun childhood.

465
01:14:59,780 --> 01:15:01,577
That's good.

466
01:15:01,660 --> 01:15:06,450
All right. Six more like that
and I'll get started on the fuses.

467
01:15:16,140 --> 01:15:18,529
He'll find us, won't he?

468
01:15:20,660 --> 01:15:22,651
Probably.

469
01:15:39,300 --> 01:15:41,894
It'll never be over, will it?

470
01:15:49,500 --> 01:15:51,695
Look at me. I'm shaking.

471
01:15:52,660 --> 01:15:57,654
Some legend.
You must be pretty disappointed.

472
01:15:58,620 --> 01:16:02,215
No, I'm not.

473
01:16:10,780 --> 01:16:14,819
Kyle, the women in your time,

474
01:16:15,900 --> 01:16:18,095
what are they like?

475
01:16:19,900 --> 01:16:21,891
Good fighters.

476
01:16:21,980 --> 01:16:24,574
That's not what I meant.

477
01:16:26,060 --> 01:16:28,449
Was there someone special?

478
01:16:29,820 --> 01:16:31,617
Someone...?

479
01:16:31,700 --> 01:16:33,895
A girl, you know.

480
01:16:35,300 --> 01:16:37,291
No.

481
01:16:39,500 --> 01:16:41,491
Never.

482
01:16:42,500 --> 01:16:44,491
Never?

483
01:16:48,820 --> 01:16:52,017
I'm sorry. I'm so sorry.

484
01:16:55,020 --> 01:16:57,011
So much pain.

485
01:16:58,100 --> 01:17:02,412
Pain can be controlled.
You just disconnect it.

486
01:17:02,940 --> 01:17:05,738
So you feel nothing?

487
01:17:09,700 --> 01:17:12,692
John Connor gave me
a picture of you once.

488
01:17:14,260 --> 01:17:17,252
I didn't know why at the time.

489
01:17:17,340 --> 01:17:23,131
It was very old, torn, faded.

490
01:17:26,620 --> 01:17:32,889
You were young like you are now.
You seemed just a little sad.

491
01:17:33,900 --> 01:17:37,939
I used to always wonder
what you were thinking at that moment.

492
01:17:38,020 --> 01:17:41,410
I memorised every line,

493
01:17:41,500 --> 01:17:43,491
every curve.

494
01:17:46,700 --> 01:17:50,409
I came across time for you, Sarah.

495
01:17:50,500 --> 01:17:54,573
I love you. I always have.

496
01:18:11,700 --> 01:18:14,089
I shouldn't have said that.

497
01:19:40,660 --> 01:19:43,254
Think fast.

498
01:21:08,140 --> 01:21:10,131
Trade places.

499
01:21:20,020 --> 01:21:23,535
Faster. Drive faster.

500
01:22:11,100 --> 01:22:14,172
Kyle. Oh, my God.

501
01:23:18,980 --> 01:23:21,369
You stay here.

502
01:23:43,220 --> 01:23:45,211
Let's get outta here.

503
01:23:47,860 --> 01:23:50,055
Get out.

504
01:24:05,500 --> 01:24:08,333
Kyle. Come on. Get up.

505
01:24:17,700 --> 01:24:19,053
Come on.

506
01:24:20,060 --> 01:24:22,858
Help me. Get out. Get out.

507
01:24:45,980 --> 01:24:48,414
Don't stop. Run.

508
01:26:55,180 --> 01:26:57,375
Sarah.

509
01:26:57,460 --> 01:26:59,257
Kyle.

510
01:27:08,260 --> 01:27:11,252
We did it, Kyle. We got him.

511
01:27:30,020 --> 01:27:31,214
No.

512
01:28:26,540 --> 01:28:28,019
Wait.

513
01:28:32,340 --> 01:28:34,695
What are you doing?

514
01:28:34,780 --> 01:28:37,055
Cover, so he can't track us.

515
01:28:47,100 --> 01:28:49,489
Come on.

516
01:28:50,340 --> 01:28:53,810
No. Kyle. Come on.

517
01:28:54,540 --> 01:28:57,612
- Come on.
- Leave me here.

518
01:29:06,900 --> 01:29:11,371
Move it, Reese. On your feet, soldier.

519
01:29:11,460 --> 01:29:14,816
On your feet. Move it.

520
01:30:23,900 --> 01:30:25,891
Get back. Go back.

521
01:30:58,820 --> 01:31:00,651
- Run, Sarah.
- No.

522
01:31:00,740 --> 01:31:02,458
Run.

523
01:31:05,540 --> 01:31:07,337
Run.

524
01:31:07,420 --> 01:31:09,980
Come on, motherfucker.

525
01:32:57,780 --> 01:32:59,577
No.

526
01:34:53,340 --> 01:34:55,535
You're terminated, fucker.

527
01:35:50,540 --> 01:35:52,531
Kyle.

528
01:36:12,180 --> 01:36:15,570
Seven. November 10. Where was I?

529
01:36:16,700 --> 01:36:20,375
What's difficult is trying to decide
what to tell you and what not to.

530
01:36:20,820 --> 01:36:25,018
But I guess I have a while before you're
old enough to understand these tapes.

531
01:36:25,100 --> 01:36:29,890
They're more for me at this point
just so that I can get it straight.

532
01:37:04,340 --> 01:37:06,331
Fill her up.

533
01:37:09,580 --> 01:37:12,140
Should I tell you about your father?

534
01:37:12,220 --> 01:37:14,415
Boy, that's a tough one.

535
01:37:15,820 --> 01:37:21,292
Will it effect your decision to send him
here, knowing that he is your father?

536
01:37:22,180 --> 01:37:24,978
If you don't send Kyle, you can never be.

537
01:37:25,060 --> 01:37:28,052
God, a person could go crazy
thinking about this.

538
01:37:31,580 --> 01:37:34,890
I suppose I will tell you.

539
01:37:34,980 --> 01:37:37,175
I owe him that.

540
01:37:39,220 --> 01:37:41,609
Maybe it'll help if...

541
01:37:41,700 --> 01:37:46,694
you know that in the few hours
that we had together,

542
01:37:48,380 --> 01:37:51,019
we loved a lifetime's worth.

543
01:38:03,060 --> 01:38:06,689
- What did he just say?
- He says you're very beautiful.

544
01:38:06,780 --> 01:38:09,772
He is ashamed to ask you
for five dollars for this picture.

545
01:38:09,860 --> 01:38:13,091
But if he doesn't, his father will beat him.

546
01:38:13,180 --> 01:38:16,092
Pretty good hustle, kid.

547
01:38:31,540 --> 01:38:33,690
What did he just say?

548
01:38:33,780 --> 01:38:36,772
He said there's a storm coming in.

549
01:38:40,100 --> 01:38:42,091
I know.

550
